-- ============================================================================
-- Script:    0063_mac765_44726_pineapple_branch_split.sql
-- Status:    DRAFT, STAGED ONLY. Filed from
--            [<TRACKER_URL>issues/MAC-765](<TRACKER_URL>issues/MAC-765), the v1.9.0
--            successor lane to MAC-761. v1.8.0 is published
--            (`origin/main` = `v1.8.0^{}` = `3fbaada`), so the MAC-765
--            hold ("do not start until v1.8.0 is pushed and tagged") is
--            discharged and this touches a file inside the certified
--            tree deliberately.
--
--            Applying this draft to canonical is a CEO/board ruling.
--
-- Slot:      0063, allocated by DISPATCH CLAIM.
--              python3 scripts/next_migration_slot.py
--            reports `next free slot : 0063` at write time. The naive
--            `ls db/migrations/` answer (0061) is WRONG: 0061 is CLAIMED
--            by MAC-742 (geography, deferred to v2.0 by CEO ruling; the
--            standing "do NOT apply 0061" rule is untouched here) and
--            0062 is CLAIMED by MAC-752. Gaps 0046/0047/0050/0053/0056/
--            0058 are contested or claimed and are NOT auto-reused.
--            RE-SCANNED AT COMMIT TIME per the mig-0049 / mig-0055
--            standing rule — a slot verified at WRITE time is not
--            verified at COMMIT time. Commit-time result:
--
--              0063  CLAIMED   MAC-765
--                      [DRAFT] db/migrations/_drafts/0063_mac765_...draft
--              next free slot       : 0064
--
--            Read that carefully before concluding the slot moved. The
--            scanner reads `db/migrations/_drafts/`, so once THIS file
--            exists it reports 0063 as claimed BY THIS LANE and hands
--            out 0064 next. That is a self-claim, not a collision: the
--            claimant is MAC-765. The generator is poisoned by its own
--            output, so `next free slot` can never re-confirm the slot
--            a written draft already holds — check the CLAIMANT, not
--            the `next free slot` line. No other lane claims 0063.
--
-- schema_version: NOT bumped (data-only in-place refine; no DDL). Stays
--            at 35.
--
-- Purpose:   Restore Hak5 SSID coverage by dropping ONE alternation
--            branch from row 44726.
--
--                before  (?i)^(pineapple|hak5|wifi[_-]?pineapple).*
--                after   (?i)^(hak5|wifi[_-]?pineapple).*
--
--            Row 44726 carries THREE alternation branches in ONE row.
--            The export-side FP hold is ROW-LEVEL: at
--            `db/validation/export_lynceus.py:1149-1151` the exporter
--            takes `_strict_base(branch)` for each branch and, on the
--            first branch whose strict base is in
--            `_SSID_PATTERN_FP_HOLD_STEMS`, does `return None` for the
--            WHOLE ROW. Holding the bare `pineapple` stem (MAC-761, CEO
--            Option A) therefore withdrew its two innocent co-branches
--            with it, and Hak5 shipped in ZERO Lynceus feeds at v1.8.0:
--
--                'hak5'          | ssid_pattern | Hak5 hacking_tool
--                'pineapple'     | ssid_pattern | Hak5 hacking_tool
--                'wifipineapple' | ssid_pattern | Hak5 hacking_tool
--
--            The hold-set cannot express "hold one branch": it is keyed
--            on the strict base and scoped to the row. Only a DATA
--            change can split the row, which is why MAC-761 deferred it
--            here rather than widening the hold-set.
--
--            Measured against the REAL emitter (not predicted):
--              before  _ssid_pattern_to_substring(44726) -> None
--              after   _ssid_pattern_to_substring(44726) -> ['hak5',
--                                                           'wifipineapple']
--            Bare `pineapple` is NOT emitted after the split: the strict
--            bases of the two surviving branches are `hak5` and `wifi`
--            (the latter stops at the `[` metachar), and the emitted
--            stem for the second branch is the delimiter-collapsed
--            `wifipineapple`.
--
-- Shape:     IN-PLACE `identifier` UPDATE, not a supersession. Precedent
--            is section (B) of
--            `db/migrations/0038_mac527_ssid_pattern_fp_magnet_refine_demote.sql`,
--            which refines 8 `ssid_pattern` rows in place for exactly
--            this FP-magnet class (e.g. id 39613
--            `(?i)^(msab|xry)[_-]?.*` -> `(?i)^(msab_|msab-|xry_|xry-).*`).
--            A refine keeps the row's id, cites, confidence and
--            provenance intact; the branch drop is recorded in `notes`.
--            Idempotent by construction: the UPDATE matches only the
--            verbatim original value, so a second run matches 0 rows.
--
-- superseded_by SEMANTICS — stated explicitly per the tri-semantic rule
--            (NULL = active / <other_id> = successor / self-loop =
--            withdrawn): this draft uses NONE of them. `superseded_by`
--            is NOT written. Row 44726 stays ACTIVE and stays itself;
--            only its `identifier` narrows. No row is withdrawn, no row
--            is folded, no row is created.
--
-- Confidence: deliberately UNCHANGED (75). This is a precision refine,
--            not a demotion — the surviving branches are the
--            HIGH-precision ones. There is no §8.3 corroboration uplift
--            either: the independent-source basis was not re-adjudicated,
--            and one source issuing the same row is hub-and-spoke, not
--            value-level corroboration.
--
-- Cite faithfulness: `source_excerpt` on row 44726 retains the VERBATIM
--            upstream pattern `"(?i)^(pineapple|hak5|wifi[_-]?pineapple).*"`
--            and is NOT rewritten. The canonical `identifier` now
--            deliberately diverges from its cite, exactly as mig-0038's
--            refines do. The divergence and its reason are recorded in
--            `notes.$.mac765_branch_split` so a later audit reading the
--            excerpt against the identifier finds the ruling instead of
--            a discrepancy.
--
-- Scope:     1 row updated in place. 0 rows superseded. 0 rows created.
--            Net ACTIVE count change: 0 (asserted below as a DELTA
--            against an apply-time baseline, not as a pinned absolute).
--
-- Fan-out:   re-queried at write time — 0 rows reference 44726 via
--            `superseded_by`, and 0 via `paired_identifier_id`.
--
-- Export-side hold: `pineapple` is RETAINED in the three
--            `_SSID_PATTERN_FP_HOLD_STEMS` mirrors
--            (`db/validation/export_lynceus.py`,
--            `db/validation/coverage_matrix.py`,
--            `operator_review/MAC-532/fp_magnet_verify.py`) as
--            forward-looking defense in depth. MAC-765 required this be
--            decided explicitly rather than left open. Measured: after
--            the split, removing `pineapple` from the hold set changes
--            the emitted output of row 44726 by NOTHING (byte-identical
--            `['hak5', 'wifipineapple']`), because no surviving branch
--            has strict base `pineapple`. So retention costs zero
--            coverage today and still fails closed if a future harvest
--            re-admits a bare `pineapple`-based row. Retention is a
--            deny-list policy statement, not a decorative assertion.
--
-- APPLY ORDER — MANDATORY, and it is NOT optional hygiene. Applying this
--            migration WITHOUT first regenerating the MAC-45 coverage
--            matrix makes the Lynceus export HALT. Measured, not
--            predicted (MAC-765, apply rehearsal on a DB copy):
--
--              $ export_lynceus.py --db <split.db>          # stale matrix
--              Halt: argus_export.json: row id=44726 writer-classified
--              as None but MAC-45 drop_assignments map says
--              'ssid_pattern_fp_hold' — STOP-THE-LINE per dispatch
--              (do NOT silently re-tally).
--
--            Cause: `extraction_outputs/mac45/coverage_matrix_report.json`
--            is a TRACKED, FROZEN reconciliation baseline that pins
--            44726 into the `ssid_pattern_fp_hold` drop bin. After the
--            split the writer correctly classifies 44726 as a SURVIVOR,
--            and `export_lynceus.py::_reconcile` halts on the
--            disagreement. That halt is the gate working as designed —
--            it is not a defect in this migration and MUST NOT be
--            worked around by editing the map by hand.
--
--            Correct order (the standing matrix -> lynceus -> behavioral
--            rule, which this migration makes load-bearing):
--              1. apply this migration
--              2. python3 db/validation/coverage_matrix.py   # regen map
--              3. python3 db/validation/export_lynceus.py    # regen feed
--
--            Regen is MINIMAL and was verified non-vacuous: at the
--            pre-apply state the regenerated map is IDENTICAL to the
--            committed one, and post-apply the ONLY differing id is
--            44726, moving 'ssid_pattern_fp_hold' -> survivor. The
--            `ssid_pattern_fp_hold` bin goes 7 -> 6 in BOTH tallies and
--            `pre_active_count` stays 43126 (delta 0).
--
-- CP7 / high-confidence: this migration restores Hak5 to the STANDARD
--            feed ONLY. Row 44726 carries `geographic_scope IS NULL`,
--            and `_passes_geographic_filter` returns
--            `not is_high_confidence` for an empty scope, so the row
--            passes standard and FAILS high-confidence. Measured
--            end-to-end: standard 1014 -> 1016 (+2, both Hak5),
--            high-confidence 504 -> 504 (+0, Hak5 stays at ZERO).
--            "Hak5 ships again" is therefore true of the standard feed
--            and still FALSE of the high-confidence feed. Closing that
--            gap is a geography question (MAC-742 / 0061), which is
--            explicitly out of scope here and deferred to v2.0.
--
-- Reversibility: to revert, restore the identifier verbatim and drop the
--            notes key:
--              UPDATE identifiers
--                 SET identifier = '(?i)^(pineapple|hak5|wifi[_-]?pineapple).*',
--                     notes = json_remove(notes, '$.mac765_branch_split')
--               WHERE id = 44726;
--            No row is deleted, no confidence altered, every cite
--            retained (§11 #1).
--
-- Re-apply safety: the UPDATE pins the verbatim BEFORE value and
--            `superseded_by IS NULL`, and the strict pre-guard requires
--            EXACTLY 1 such row — a second run finds 0, CHECK(ok=1)
--            fails, and `.bail on` rolls the whole transaction back with
--            zero mutation. Both guard arms are present per
--            scripts/check_migration_guards.py: arm 1 `.bail on` (CLI)
--            and arm 2 the per-write
--            `AND (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM _mig0063_pre) = 1` (portable,
--            for the conn.executescript() apply path where `.bail on`
--            is a syntax error).
-- ============================================================================

.bail on

BEGIN TRANSACTION;

-- ---- apply-time baseline capture -------------------------------------------
-- Captured INSIDE the transaction so the post-condition compares against the
-- state this migration actually started from, not a number pinned at write
-- time. A pinned absolute goes stale the moment any other lane lands.
CREATE TEMP TABLE _mig0063_baseline AS
  SELECT (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM identifiers WHERE superseded_by IS NULL)
           AS active_before,
         (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM identifiers
           WHERE identifier_type = 'ssid_pattern' AND superseded_by IS NULL)
           AS ssid_active_before;

CREATE TEMP TABLE _mig0063_conf_before AS
  SELECT id, confidence, superseded_by, source_excerpt
    FROM identifiers WHERE id = 44726;

-- ---- strict pre-condition guard (all-or-nothing) ---------------------------
-- (1) exactly 1 row 44726, ssid_pattern, ACTIVE, carrying the verbatim
--     three-branch identifier;
-- (2) its notes are valid JSON (so the json_set below cannot corrupt them);
-- (3) NO active row already carries the post-split identifier — this migration
--     must not create a duplicate emitted key;
-- (4) fan-out is zero on both reference columns.
CREATE TEMP TABLE _mig0063_pre (ok INTEGER CHECK (ok = 1));
INSERT INTO _mig0063_pre(ok) SELECT CASE WHEN (
      (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM identifiers
        WHERE id = 44726 AND identifier_type = 'ssid_pattern'
          AND identifier = '(?i)^(pineapple|hak5|wifi[_-]?pineapple).*'
          AND superseded_by IS NULL) = 1
  AND (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM identifiers
        WHERE id = 44726 AND json_valid(notes) = 1) = 1
  AND (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM identifiers
        WHERE identifier = '(?i)^(hak5|wifi[_-]?pineapple).*'
          AND superseded_by IS NULL) = 0
  AND (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM identifiers WHERE superseded_by = 44726) = 0
  AND (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM identifiers WHERE paired_identifier_id = 44726) = 0
) THEN 1 ELSE 0 END;

-- ==== The split: drop the bare `pineapple` branch from row 44726 ============
UPDATE identifiers SET
  identifier = '(?i)^(hak5|wifi[_-]?pineapple).*',
  notes = json_set(notes, '$.mac765_branch_split', json_object(
    'audit_issue','MAC-765','source_issue','MAC-761',
    'mig','0063_mac765_44726_pineapple_branch_split',
    'mechanism','in_place_alternation_branch_drop',
    'shape','refine_not_supersession',
    'identifier_before','(?i)^(pineapple|hak5|wifi[_-]?pineapple).*',
    'identifier_after','(?i)^(hak5|wifi[_-]?pineapple).*',
    'branch_dropped','pineapple',
    'branches_kept','hak5,wifi[_-]?pineapple',
    'emitted_before','(row FP-held: _ssid_pattern_to_substring returned None)',
    'emitted_after','hak5,wifipineapple',
    'source_excerpt_divergence','INTENTIONAL. source_excerpt retains the verbatim upstream three-branch pattern and is not rewritten; the canonical identifier deliberately narrows away from its cite, per the mig-0038 (MAC-527) refine precedent. This key is the record of that divergence.',
    'reason','The export-side FP hold is ROW-level, not branch-level: export_lynceus.py takes _strict_base(branch) per alternation branch and returns None for the WHOLE row on the first held base. Holding the bare 9-char English noun `pineapple` (MAC-761 CEO Option A) therefore also withdrew the innocent co-branches `hak5` and `wifi[_-]?pineapple`, so Hak5 -- the canonical rogue-AP vendor Lynceus exists to catch -- shipped in zero v1.8.0 feeds. The hold-set cannot express a single-branch hold, so the correction is a data change. Dropping the bare branch restores the two precise branches while keeping bare `pineapple` out of the emitted set.',
    'fp_hold_disposition','RETAINED in all three _SSID_PATTERN_FP_HOLD_STEMS mirrors as forward-looking defense in depth. Measured no-op post-split: no surviving branch has strict base `pineapple`, so removing it from the hold set leaves row 44726 emitting a byte-identical [hak5, wifipineapple].',
    'confidence_uplift','none','date','2026-08-20'))
WHERE id = 44726 AND identifier_type = 'ssid_pattern'
  AND identifier = '(?i)^(pineapple|hak5|wifi[_-]?pineapple).*'
  AND superseded_by IS NULL
  AND (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM _mig0063_pre) = 1;

-- ---- strict post-condition guard -------------------------------------------
-- (1) DEFECT CLASS GONE — no active row carries the three-branch identifier.
-- (2) exactly 1 active row carries the post-split identifier, and it is 44726.
-- (3) row 44726 is STILL ACTIVE — this is a refine, so superseded_by stays
--     NULL. Explicitly NOT a self-loop and NOT a successor pointer.
-- (4) notes stayed valid JSON and the audit key landed and is readable.
-- (5) CONFIDENCE and SOURCE_EXCERPT UNTOUCHED — the exact multiset of
--     (id, confidence, superseded_by, source_excerpt) is unchanged, which is
--     what makes the cite-faithfulness claim above checkable rather than
--     asserted.
-- (6) NO COLLATERAL, AS A DELTA: the active row count and the active
--     ssid_pattern row count are each EQUAL to their apply-time baseline
--     (delta 0). Asserted against _mig0063_baseline, never against a pinned
--     absolute such as 43126 -- a pinned number would silently pass or
--     silently fail depending on what else landed first.
CREATE TEMP TABLE _mig0063_post (ok INTEGER CHECK (ok = 1));
INSERT INTO _mig0063_post(ok) SELECT CASE WHEN (
      (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM identifiers
        WHERE identifier = '(?i)^(pineapple|hak5|wifi[_-]?pineapple).*'
          AND superseded_by IS NULL) = 0
  AND (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM identifiers
        WHERE identifier = '(?i)^(hak5|wifi[_-]?pineapple).*'
          AND superseded_by IS NULL) = 1
  AND (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM identifiers
        WHERE id = 44726
          AND identifier = '(?i)^(hak5|wifi[_-]?pineapple).*'
          AND identifier_type = 'ssid_pattern'
          AND superseded_by IS NULL) = 1
  AND (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM identifiers
        WHERE id = 44726 AND superseded_by = 44726) = 0
  AND (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM identifiers
        WHERE id = 44726 AND json_valid(notes) = 1) = 1
  AND (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM identifiers
        WHERE id = 44726
          AND json_extract(notes, '$.mac765_branch_split.branch_dropped')
              = 'pineapple') = 1
  AND (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM (
          SELECT id, confidence, superseded_by, source_excerpt
            FROM _mig0063_conf_before
          EXCEPT
          SELECT id, confidence, superseded_by, source_excerpt
            FROM identifiers WHERE id = 44726)) = 0
  AND (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM (
          SELECT id, confidence, superseded_by, source_excerpt
            FROM identifiers WHERE id = 44726
          EXCEPT
          SELECT id, confidence, superseded_by, source_excerpt
            FROM _mig0063_conf_before)) = 0
  AND (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM _mig0063_conf_before) = 1
  AND (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM identifiers WHERE superseded_by IS NULL)
      = (SELECT active_before FROM _mig0063_baseline)
  AND (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM identifiers
        WHERE identifier_type = 'ssid_pattern' AND superseded_by IS NULL)
      = (SELECT ssid_active_before FROM _mig0063_baseline)
) THEN 1 ELSE 0 END;

DROP TABLE _mig0063_pre;
DROP TABLE _mig0063_post;
DROP TABLE _mig0063_baseline;
DROP TABLE _mig0063_conf_before;

COMMIT;

-- Post-apply expected state:
--   Row 44726 stays ACTIVE, id unchanged, confidence unchanged (75),
--   source_excerpt unchanged, superseded_by still NULL.
--   identifier: (?i)^(pineapple|hak5|wifi[_-]?pineapple).*
--            -> (?i)^(hak5|wifi[_-]?pineapple).*
--   Active identifiers: UNCHANGED (delta 0). Active ssid_pattern rows:
--   UNCHANGED (delta 0). No schema change (schema_version stays 35).
--   Emitted ssid_pattern substrings: row 44726 moves from FP-held (None)
--   to ['hak5', 'wifipineapple'], so the emitted-row count rises by 1 and
--   the distinct-substring count by 2. Bare `pineapple` stays OUT.
--   Feed deltas were MEASURED by end-to-end apply rehearsal on a DB copy
--   (never against canonical), running the REAL coverage_matrix.py and
--   export_lynceus.py in that order, and are reported on MAC-765:
--
--     standard          argus_export.json                1014 -> 1016  (+2)
--     high-confidence   argus_export_high_confidence.json 504 ->  504  (+0)
--
--   The corpus multiset diff on the standard feed is GAINED=2 / LOST=0,
--   and BOTH gained entries are Hak5:
--     + ('hak5',          'ssid_pattern', 'Hak5 hacking_tool')
--     + ('wifipineapple', 'ssid_pattern', 'Hak5 hacking_tool')
--   so the union delta EQUALS the per-entity delta and hides no
--   attribution. Per-entity Hak5 standard: 0 -> 2. High-confidence
--   Hak5: 0 -> 0 (CP7 NULL geo — see above). Bare `pineapple` is absent
--   from both feeds and from argus_export.csv after the split.
--
--   argus_export.csv is NOT a feed-delta instrument for this change: it
--   publishes the full active set at threshold=0, so 44726 was always
--   present and only its `identifier` text changes. Line count is
--   47491 before AND after.
